I'd like to start to get started  everything at MIT runs late but we're  only about five minutes so I think  technically we're right on time so I'd  like to welcome all of you to the annual  McGovern spring symposium and this one  is on computations from synapses to  systems so I have to first say thank you  very very much to Laura McGovern and the  McGovern family for their support of our  Institute and in particular for making  this symposium possible and I'd also  like to extend a very deep thank you to  the headquarters staff at McGovern  they've been incredible in helping me  the most inexperienced faculty member  here at MIT organized this very very  complicated symposium so everything that  goes right today is down to them  everything that goes wrong today is  likely down to me so I'd also like to  say thank you very very much to our  speakers who have travelled a collective  20,000 miles from Canada the West Coast  the East Coast the UK and Europe to be  with here be with us here today and no  less on Mother's Day weekend we really  appreciate you coming to share your work  with us today and we're really looking  forward to hearing all about your take  on computations from synapses to systems  so what I want to do in in just a minute  or two is to pose two challenges to sort  of frame frame what's going to happen  today the first is something of a  definition of terms this idea or this  concept of a computation is a very  nebulous one something that I've learned  in my first year here at MIT is that  computations mean very different things  to different people some of us think  about computations at the level of  single cells say orientation tuning in  the visual cortex many of us think about  computations at a much higher level more  cognitive processes like goal goal  directed decision-making and valuation  and so I'd like for us for the speakers  and for the audience to think about what  counts as a computation where do we draw  the boundaries around different kinds of  computations  what does it mean to have a computation  at this level versus another level and  how do we merge those levels or move  between those levels so I'd really like  to encourage the audience to push our  speakers a little bit on some of these  issues so the other thing I'd like to  think about as we hear all these  exciting talks today is an issue about  goals what does success look like as we  study neural computations Tom Insel  former director of the National  Institutes of mental health and now at  Google just visited us here at MIT not  very long ago I had a chance to meet  with him and after talking with him  about you know what I was doing in my  lab and you know kind of questions I was  asking he asked me you know what the  success look like for you mark in five  or ten years how will you know that  you've answered your problem and I  really wanted to have a great answer for  him and I didn't and was really  disappointed about it he mentioned that  he's asked this question to a lot of  people both in brain science and  artificial intelligence and no one seems  to have a particularly great answer  other than well we'll know it when we  see it that may well be true but it's  not tremendously satisfying so it's  something I'd like us to think about is  as we're doing all this work on neural  circuits and computation and trying to  figure out you know what is it about  computation in the brain that gives rise  to the mind how can we think about sort  of milestones on our way to the thing  that we we'll know when we see and let's  be sure that when it actually shows up  we will know it so with that I would  like to thank you all again so much for  coming and I'll turn it over to Michael  fee our first session chair
 

